                <h2>When Do You Need Account Tier Management</h2>
<p>When your team manages more than 5 advertising accounts, managing them through spreadsheets or memory alone starts to become chaotic. Specific warning signs include: an account suddenly runs out of balance with nobody noticing, materials are repeatedly uploaded across accounts making performance tracking impossible, or monthly budget allocation has completely deviated from plan by month-end.</p>
<p>Common multi-account scenarios fall into three categories: separate accounts for the same product across different platforms (Meta, TikTok, Google); multiple campaigns on the same platform requiring isolated testing; or agency-client or multi-team shared account resources. Regardless of the scenario, tier management should be planned before account numbers grow, not after problems arise.</p>
<h2>How to Define Tier Standards</h2>
<p>Account tiers are typically divided by two dimensions: business importance and financial risk level.</p>
<p>Business dimension separates core accounts from new test accounts. Core accounts are stable performers with measurable ROI where teams invest more optimization effort; new test accounts validate new creatives, targeting, or markets with limited budgets and higher volatility acceptance.</p>
<p>Financial dimension separates high-spend from low-spend accounts. High-spend accounts may consume thousands of dollars daily, so misoperations or abnormal traffic can quickly accumulate losses; low-spend accounts have more manageable止损 costs even when issues occur.</p>
<p>A simple tiering reference:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tier A (Core High-Spend): $2000+ daily budget, flagship products, stable ROI</li>
<li>Tier B (Core Low-Spend): $500-2000 daily budget, mature campaigns</li>
<li>Tier C (Test High-Risk): New creatives, new markets, under $500 budget</li>
<li>Tier D (Backup or Dormant): Paused campaigns preserving data, or long-inactive</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to Allocate Account Permissions</h2>
<p>The core principle for permission settings is "minimum necessary permissions." Media buyers don't need recharge passwords; finance staff don't need to operate creatives.</p>
<p>A common three-level permission structure:</p>
<ul>
<li>Admin permissions: account setup, recharge, permission allocation, typically held by team leads or finance</li>
<li>Operation permissions: campaign settings, material uploads, campaign on/off, held by executing media buyers</li>
<li>Read-only permissions: data viewing, report export, for other departments needing data without operation access</li>
</ul>
<p>When using platform tools like Meta Business Manager or TikTok Business Center for multi-person permission management, create individual work accounts for each media buyer rather than sharing login credentials. This enables quick permission revocation during personnel changes while preserving operation logs for audit trails.</p>
<h2>How to Allocate Budget and Recharge</h2>
<p>After tier implementation, budget allocation strategies should also be tiered. Tier A accounts use fixed budgets with real-time monitoring to ensure uninterrupted core delivery; Tier C accounts use test budget caps with automatic pausing or alerts upon reaching preset spend thresholds.</p>
<p>Recharge cycles should align with account tiers: Tier A accounts maintain balance no lower than 7-day budget, with balance alerts configured; Tier C accounts recharge on-demand to avoid capital stagnation. Recharge records across multiple accounts need unified management to avoid double-charging one account while another is forgotten.</p>
<h2>How to Handle Multi-Account Financial Reconciliation</h2>
<p>Financial reconciliation is the implementation ground for account tier management. Reconciliation frequency adjusts by account tier: daily for Tier A, weekly for Tier B, and on-demand for Tier C.</p>
<p>Reconciliation focuses on three consistencies: platform backend spend matches account balance changes; recharge amounts match recharge records; campaign spend matches conversion data. For teams managing over 10 accounts, a unified financial spreadsheet template is recommended, including fields for account name, platform, recharge date, recharge amount, spend amount, and balance.</p>
<p>Special reminder: If your team uses agents or third-party platforms for recharges, additional verification is needed for recharge arrival timing (delays sometimes occur) and whether exchange rate conversions are accurate.</p>
<h2>How to Respond Quickly to Account Anomalies</h2>
<p>A hidden value of tier management is rapid problem account identification. When an account shows abnormal spend or traffic fluctuations, the team can immediately determine which tier it belongs to, retrieve corresponding operation records, and identify responsible personnel.</p>
<p>Response protocols for each tier are recommended: Tier A account anomalies trigger immediate Campaign pause and leader notification; Tier C account anomalies require evaluation of whether investigation is worthwhile, prioritizing Tier A account stability.</p>
<p>Establish weekly account health reports covering spend trends, ROI fluctuations, and creative replacement records across all tiers. This report serves both as a review tool and handover documentation during personnel changes.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Account tier management isn't exclusive to large teams. Even with only 3-5 accounts, defining tier standards, permission boundaries, and financial reconciliation processes in advance prevents significant rework during team expansion. Efficiency gaps in multi-account delivery often depend less on optimization skills and more on management granularity.</p>
